 ## Overview
-Triplanar texture projection maps a 2D texture onto a 3D surface by applying planar mapping in three perpendicular planes (XY, XZ, YZ) and interpolating the results based on the surface normal's alignment [S2]. It solves the problem of texture stretching that occurs with single-axis planar mapping on complex or curved surfaces, such as spheres, by ensuring the texture orientation adapts to the local geometry [S2]. Unlike its siblings, it avoids visible seams and stretching by weighting three orthogonal projections, making it suitable for shading complex volumetric surfaces [S2].
